{
 "text": "Noise-cancelling headphones erase sound by adding more sound. On a plane, the cabin hums at about 80 decibels. That hum is a pressure wave, air squeezing and stretching. Microphones on your headphones catch it. A chip flips the wave upside down, so every squeeze becomes a stretch. The speaker plays this inverted wave into your ear. The two waves cancel out. That's destructive interference. The hum keeps changing, so some headphones adjust 50,000 times a second. But the flight attendant's voice still gets through. Voices are higher. Their waves are shorter, so the tiniest delay ruins the match.",
